        "fr-extract.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "a27bddd1-363e-3ed9-a23b-b904155faf24",
                "word": "ambage",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "fr",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "d59f6a43-9c68-4991-b97a-f09eb78b90d1",
                        "entry_id": "a27bddd1-363e-3ed9-a23b-b904155faf24",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "ambages"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "0941ac36-73a9-3097-9e73-c30ddb4b41f0",
                        "entry_id": "a27bddd1-363e-3ed9-a23b-b904155faf24",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Détour, sinuosité."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "poetic"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "6612891d-beb7-4781-b514-dd5c288230a9",
                                "sense_id": "0941ac36-73a9-3097-9e73-c30ddb4b41f0",
                                "text": "Ce procès traînait dans les délais, dans le lacis inextricable de la procédure, arrêté par les ambages d’un odieux avocat ; tandis que Pierrette calomniée languissait et souffrait les plus épouvantables douleurs connues en médecine."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "9ef78442-246c-44c7-8c1b-ab9662332f8b",
                                "sense_id": "0941ac36-73a9-3097-9e73-c30ddb4b41f0",
                                "text": "Tu décris un ambage aux lieux que de tomber (comme cette phrase)."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "d84f3b41-969d-3350-bdbd-517436d233cb",
                        "entry_id": "a27bddd1-363e-3ed9-a23b-b904155faf24",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Complexification du discours, volontaire ou involontaire, ayant pour résultat que le sens n’apparaisse pas directement."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "figuratively"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "b2fa7750-87b4-47c4-b963-9062f29628a9",
                                "sense_id": "d84f3b41-969d-3350-bdbd-517436d233cb",
                                "text": "Heureusement pour qui préfèrent les femmes avec ou sans Oscar (qu'ils et elles me pardonnent!), ils se le disent entre de beaux ambages!"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "7f79237c-2ebd-4dc5-98ee-f55d1b76b6c5",
                                "sense_id": "d84f3b41-969d-3350-bdbd-517436d233cb",
                                "text": "Ces plaintes qu’il [Anne-Jules de Noailles] me fit se prolongèrent quelques jours avant d’en venir au point que je sentis après qui l’avoit pressé de me les faire, et ce fut lorsqu’il y vint où l’ambage de ses discours me fit entrevoir ce qu’il se proposoit par le duc de Beauvilliers."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "565e8371-8528-443c-9a91-1e266dc3a175",
                                "sense_id": "d84f3b41-969d-3350-bdbd-517436d233cb",
                                "text": "Soudain, il a repris son air sérieux et, sans plus d’ambages, m’a demandé de lui dire ce que je savais sur la personnalité du ténor Dossegor."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "c50b2c91-a40f-4f44-8c64-eafc0897c227",
                                "sense_id": "d84f3b41-969d-3350-bdbd-517436d233cb",
                                "text": "Ces remarques voisinent avec des appréciations concernant la complexité de la construction de ces œuvres, et notamment du cycle du Lancelot-Graal : depuis les très beaux ambages de Dante (De Vulgari eloquentia I, 10), jusqu’à la cathédrale, la sparterie ou la tapisserie de F. Lot (op. cit., p. 324) ou l’enluminure irlandaise évoquée par Ch. Méla (La Reine et le Graal, Paris, Seuil, 1984, p. 325)."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "7ce6f122-02cc-4e2a-a385-cb78362ce197",
                        "source_entry_id": "a27bddd1-363e-3ed9-a23b-b904155faf24",
                        "target_lang_word": "ambiguity",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "ambiguity",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "aefef315-5542-39eb-887c-cd977f8811a3",
                                        "entry_id": "fe856398-c0d8-3ac5-a71a-62063c91bdf1",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The state of being ambiguous; the state of leaving room for more than one interpretation."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "9dba432b-8b82-4b83-bf66-4f9bf56cfaa0",
                                                "sense_id": "aefef315-5542-39eb-887c-cd977f8811a3",
                                                "text": "His speech was made with such great ambiguity that neither supporter nor opponent could be certain of his true position."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d5592aea-f392-41da-80f7-68466a37aed2",
                                                "sense_id": "aefef315-5542-39eb-887c-cd977f8811a3",
                                                "text": "Whan this ſayd monument diſcouered was / Suche a ſuauite and fragrant odoure / Aſcended from the corps by ſingular grace / Paſſyng all worldly ſwetnes and ſauour / That all there present that day and hour / Suppoſed they had ben / in the felicite / Of erthely paradiſe / without ambiguite."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "307b5f88-5d72-3050-b802-d44c87e5efdd",
                                        "entry_id": "fe856398-c0d8-3ac5-a71a-62063c91bdf1",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "An instance of this state: words or statements that are open to more than one interpretation, explanation or meaning, especially if that meaning cannot be determined from the context."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "44f83797-11ca-4ac8-a0a0-d4b938a66e69",
                                                "sense_id": "307b5f88-5d72-3050-b802-d44c87e5efdd",
                                                "text": "If two persons bore the same name, and confusion was likely to be caused, ambiguity was avoided by adding the name of the father[…]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c305ed61-1f5b-4e4c-9544-528f661375b9",
                                                "sense_id": "307b5f88-5d72-3050-b802-d44c87e5efdd",
                                                "text": "For people who would rather face a plague of locusts than permit an avoidable ambiguity, this is like having their underwear twisted. You may think we should say to such people, “Get a life.” By all means tell them that. I’m neither making these rules up nor trying to enforce them; my job in this book is to point out to you what seems to be the current state of the language and its speakers.\""
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "6e45d835-b5b2-4bda-b8f3-b368547ee0fd",
                        "source_entry_id": "a27bddd1-363e-3ed9-a23b-b904155faf24",
                        "target_lang_word": "circumlocution",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "circumlocution",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "2d2a12e3-5d59-3130-8aa4-ef8bad29ba01",
                                        "entry_id": "33e31524-3716-3fb2-ba10-54e8817659a7",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A roundabout or indirect way of speaking; thus"
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "88517415-c412-45c5-8c86-52101dd06aec",
                                                "sense_id": "2d2a12e3-5d59-3130-8aa4-ef8bad29ba01",
                                                "text": "Circumlocution is a large deſcription either to ſette forth a thyng more gorgiouſlie, or els to hyde it, if the eares cannot beare the open ſpeakyng: or when with fewe woordes we cannot open our meanyng, to ſpeake it more largely."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ced92729-821f-4f4a-a5cd-9fe24598db2b",
                                                "sense_id": "2d2a12e3-5d59-3130-8aa4-ef8bad29ba01",
                                                "text": "His Majeſty leaves the choice to your ſelf, and requires from you a direct Anſwer without circumlocution or bargaining with him […]"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "feb063e2-d5ff-305b-a04b-18e93ce1ade8",
                                        "entry_id": "33e31524-3716-3fb2-ba10-54e8817659a7",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A roundabout or indirect way of speaking; thus:",
                                            "Unnecessary use of extra words to express an idea, such as a pleonastic phrase (sometimes driven by an attempt at emphatic clarity) or a wordy substitution (the latter driven by euphemistic intent, pedagogic intent, or sometimes loquaciousness alone)."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "454c1937-9dcf-38d7-a174-7df17a8ba3a1",
                                        "entry_id": "33e31524-3716-3fb2-ba10-54e8817659a7",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A roundabout or indirect way of speaking; thus:",
                                            "Necessary use of a phrase to circumvent either a vocabulary fault (of speaker or listener) or a lexical gap, either monolingually or in translation."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c33c5b1c-9a87-4d3e-ad97-f6282236f46f",
                                                "sense_id": "454c1937-9dcf-38d7-a174-7df17a8ba3a1",
                                                "text": "A technical word, such as hyperkalemia or hypoallergenic, can be glossed for general audiences with a circumlocution, such as \"high potassium level\" or \"less likely to cause allergies\" (respectively)."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7ec1f4ff-e514-4fbc-9324-ff8c52d978e3",
                                                "sense_id": "454c1937-9dcf-38d7-a174-7df17a8ba3a1",
                                                "text": "It may seem quite straightforward to render line 15 metrically regular by means of a grammatical trick: \"the style as bright as soul\". However, the stylistic feature of the adjectival compound does not occur in isolation but is employed again in the following line, \"dem Staubfaden himmelswüst\", where a grammatical circumlocution in translation would be metrically disadvantageous. Both compounds, \"seelenhell\" as well as \"himmelswüst\", should therefore be translated as compounds. When Michael Hamburger remarks that German \"lends itself to the formation of compound words in a way that English does not\" (in Celan 1988: 24), he probably refers to nouns such as \"Türspalt\" or \"Algenteilchen\"."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ceb7982c-2415-44e3-aeb8-c179bc90be35",
                                                "sense_id": "454c1937-9dcf-38d7-a174-7df17a8ba3a1",
                                                "text": "Linguistic horror vacui again, and lacuna: the blank part of a manuscript where the ink runs off the papyrus, and lexically the word missing from one language that must be bridged with circumlocution in translation."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "44915965-63bf-3c40-b931-02f6e1808415",
                                        "entry_id": "33e31524-3716-3fb2-ba10-54e8817659a7",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "An instance of such usage; a roundabout expression, whether an inadvisable one or a necessary one."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    }
